course: dictionary
lesson: 7393
title: Breathing roots
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Context means the words around a new word. They often give clues to its meaning. «Stilt roots hold mangroves up in soft mud and also take in some air, and some mangroves have snorkel-like roots called pneumatophores that stick up and take in air, because the mud has little oxygen.»
explain: The sentence says pneumatophores stick up and take in air, so a pneumatophore must be a part of a plant, a root that rises up. The clue «snorkel-like» helps too, because a snorkel is a tube that lets you breathe above water.
explain: The reason is in the sentence: «because the mud has little oxygen». Mangroves grow in mud where there is little air, so their roots reach up to breathe. A good check is to swap your meaning in: a breathing root that sticks up fits the sentence.
fact: A pneumatophore is a special root of a mangrove tree.
fact: It sticks up out of the mud or water, like a pencil, so the tree can take in air.
fact: Pneumatophores are often called breathing roots.
fact: Mangroves grow in salty mud, which has little oxygen in it.
fact: Mangrove pneumatophores are found in places such as Australia and Southeast Asia.
exercise: «Some mangroves have snorkel-like roots called pneumatophores.» What is a pneumatophore? | A stone that sinks in the sea | A fish that lives in the mud | *A special root that sticks up and takes in air | A leaf that catches sunlight
exercise: «The mud has little oxygen.» Why do pneumatophores stick up? | To catch fish | To make the mud sweeter | *To take in air | To grow new leaves
exercise: Which place is given as an example of where mangrove pneumatophores are found? | *Australia | Antarctica | The Alps | The Sahara Desert
test: Why do mangrove roots stick up out of the mud? | Because roots like to be tall | Because the mud is too hot | *Because the mud has little oxygen, so the tree needs to take in air | Because they want to catch the sun
test: What is a pneumatophore like, according to the sentence? | *A snorkel, a tube for breathing | A sofa | A bicycle wheel | A kite
exam: Using the clues «snorkel-like» and «because the mud has little oxygen», explain in your own words what a pneumatophore does. | *It is a breathing root that sticks up out of the mud so the mangrove can take in air | It is a seed that floats away on the sea | It is a leaf that makes sugar only at night | It is a stone that keeps the mud still
